---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout title="ALL-LLLM Pod — Ghost Audio">
  <main class="ghost-page">
    <a class="back" href="/projects/all-lllm-pod/">← ALL-LLLM Pod</a>

    <header class="hero">
      <p class="eyebrow">TRANSCRIPT RECONSTRUCTION</p>
      <h1>Ghost Audio</h1>
      <p class="lede">An investigation into transcript text omissions</p>

      <p>
        <strong>Ghost Audio</strong> is speech that's present in the source but so faint or buried that even a human
        struggles to hear it, causing it to vanish from the transcript without a trace. Unlike a
        standard omission—the kind counted by word error rate—it leaves no visible gap, so nothing
        signals that anything is missing. The only way to find it is by being familiar with the source.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>Why this matters:</strong> When an entire segment of a host's speech disappears,
        the transcript can distort what they said, or what they were responding to, with no signs
        of error. That directly degrades the training data, teaching models relationships and
        response patterns that never actually occurred.
      </p>

      <p>
        To illustrate this, I use two movie examples: a staged clip from <em>Star Wars</em> and a
        real one from <em>The Dark Knight</em>, found on YouTube.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section>


      <details class="accordion-block">
        <summary><span>Illustrating Ghost Audio: <em>The Empire Strikes Back (1980)</em></span></summary>

        <div class="video-block">
          <video controls>
            <source src="https://pdf-sorcerer.github.io/alp/sw_ghost.mp4" type="video/mp4" />
          </video>
          <div class="video-caption">SOURCE VIDEO · THE EMPIRE STRIKES BACK (1980)</div>
        </div>

        <div class="callout">
          <p>
            This staged example removes Vader's central reveal:
            <code>"No. I am your father."</code>
          </p>

          <p>
            Nothing appears broken in the transcript. Luke still reacts emotionally,
            but the cause of that reaction is gone. A downstream model would see Luke
            rejecting his own accusation, not responding to new information from Vader.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="comparison-grid">
          <div class="comparison-card">
            <h3>Movie Screenplay</h3>
            <div class="transcript-box">
              <p><strong>Vader:</strong> "If only you knew the power of the dark side. Obi-Wan never told you what happened to your father."</p>
              <p><strong>Luke:</strong> "He told me enough. He told me you killed him!"</p>
              <p style="border-left: 4px solid #111; padding-left: 12px;">
  <strong>Vader:</strong> "No. I am your father."
</p>
              <p><strong>Luke:</strong> "No. No. That's not true! That's impossible!"</p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="comparison-card">
            <h3>Ghost Audio Transcript</h3>
            <div class="transcript-box">
              <p><strong>Vader:</strong> "If only you knew the power of the dark side. Obi-Wan never told you what happened to your father."</p>
              <p><strong>Luke:</strong> "He told me enough. He told me you killed him!"</p>
              <p class="ghost-quote">
                <span class="ghost-icon" aria-hidden="true">👻</span>
                <span class="ghost-loop-line"><strong>Vader:</strong> "No. I am your father."</span>
              </p>
              <p><strong>Luke:</strong> "No. No. That's not true! That's impossible!"</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="accordion-block">
        <summary><span>Illustrating Ghost Audio: <em>The Dark Knight (2008)</em></span></summary>

        <div class="video-block">
          <video controls>
            <source src="https://pdf-sorcerer.github.io/alp/out.mp4" type="video/mp4" />
          </video>
          <div class="video-caption">SOURCE VIDEO · THE DARK KNIGHT (2008)</div>
        </div>

        <div class="callout">
          <p>
            YouTube ASR failed to transcribe Batman's single-word response:
            <code>"Them?"</code>
          </p>

          <p>
            The word marks the moment Batman realizes there are two hostages rather than one.
            Nothing else in the transcript makes the omission obvious.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="comparison-grid">
          <div class="comparison-card">
            <h3>Movie Screenplay</h3>
            <div class="transcript-box">
              <p><strong>Joker:</strong> "There's only minutes left, so you'll have to play my little game if you want to save one of them."</p>
              <p style="border-left: 4px solid #111; padding-left: 12px;">
  <strong>Batman:</strong> "Them?"
</p>
              <p><strong>Joker:</strong> "You know, for a while there, I thought you really were Dent."</p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="comparison-card">
            <h3>Ghost Audio Transcript</h3>
            <div class="transcript-box">
              <p><strong>Joker:</strong> "There's only minutes left, so you'll have to play my little game if you want to save one of them."</p>
              <p class="ghost-quote">
                <span class="ghost-icon" aria-hidden="true">👻</span>
                <span class="ghost-loop-line"><strong>Batman:</strong> "Them?"</span>
              </p>
              <p><strong>Joker:</strong> "You know, for a while there, I thought you really were Dent."</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </details>

      <div class="ghost-discovery">
        <p>
          While auditing Episode 136 of the All-In Podcast, I found two Ghost Audio events
          seconds apart. One had survived in an older transcript but disappeared from later,
          otherwise superior pipelines. The other existed only in the source audio.
        </p>

        <p>
          That discovery led to a month-long investigation into the acoustic stack, decoder
          behavior, and a workflow for surfacing similar omissions elsewhere in the corpus.
        </p>
      </div>

    </section>

<section>
      <h2>Discovery — Episode 136</h2>

      <p>
        In early 2026, I moved to Libsyn as the canonical source for <em>All-In Podcast</em>
        episodes and reprocessed several older episodes to check them against my existing transcripts.
      </p>

      <p>
        Episode 136 was already a known problem case for my voice classifier, which attributes speech
        to individual hosts. Its cold open is overlapping banter: the moderator, [name], was on vacation,
        and the other hosts were mocking him.
      </p>

      <p>
        About 21 seconds in, Friedberg says, <strong>“Oh, my god.”</strong> The line is clearly audible
        and appeared in an early transcript, but every later pipeline dropped it. Moments later, guest
        Brad Gerstner says the same phrase, but so quietly that I only noticed it while watching the
        source video. It appeared in no transcript at all.
      </p>

      <div class="video-block">
        <video controls>
          <source src="https://pdf-sorcerer.github.io/alp/both_ghosts.mp4" type="video/mp4" />
        </video>
      </div>

      <div class="comparison-grid">
        <div class="comparison-card">
          <h3>Ground Truth</h3>
          <div class="transcript-box">
            <p><strong>Chamath:</strong> "...shortest that's the fattest, and the fattest that's the dumbest."</p>

            <p class="ghost-marked">
              <strong>Friedberg:</strong> "Oh, my god."
            </p>

            <p><strong>Friedberg:</strong> "He's not even here. You can't do that. It's awful."</p>

            <p class="ghost-marked">
              <strong>Gerstner:</strong> "Oh, my god."
            </p>

            <p><strong>Friedberg:</strong> "That's just bullying. That's awful."</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="comparison-card">
          <h3>Ghost Audio Transcript</h3>
          <div class="transcript-box">
            <p><strong>Chamath:</strong> "...shortest that's the fattest, and the fattest that's the dumbest."</p>

            <p class="ghost-quote">
              <span class="ghost-icon" aria-hidden="true">👻</span>
              <span class="ghost-loop-line"><strong>Friedberg:</strong> "Oh, my god."</span>
            </p>

            <p><strong>Friedberg:</strong> "He's not even here. You can't do that. It's awful."</p>

            <p class="ghost-quote">
              <span class="ghost-icon" aria-hidden="true">👻</span>
              <span class="ghost-loop-line"><strong>Gerstner:</strong> "Oh, my god."</span>
            </p>

            <p><strong>Friedberg:</strong> "That's just bullying. That's awful."</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>
        Friedberg’s omission was detectable because an earlier version had the line. Gerstner’s was not.
        Nothing in the transcripts pointed to it, and I can’t say how many others are missing.
      </p>

</section>

    <section>
      <h2>Process of Elimination: Ruling Out Audio Failure</h2>

      <p>
        I started by working with chatbots to understand the atomics of AI transcription—topics I hadn’t
        really explored beyond being a meat proxy for models and copy-pasting code to process episodes
        at scale. Then I probed each layer of the pipeline to find where the speech was disappearing.
      </p>

      <p>
        First, I wanted to know whether the missing speech was actually in the audio. Sounds silly, but
        I wanted to visualize the audio window and rule out simple explanations. One was whether Chamath’s
        voice immediately before Friedberg was effectively outcompeting the quieter speech; another was
        whether segmentation was dropping it before the decoder ever had a chance.
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>VAD:</strong> I varied onset and offset thresholds to test whether segmentation was dropping the lines. <em>[Confirm outcome.]</em></li>
        <li><strong>Energy:</strong> RMS showed energy above the noise floor in both regions.</li>
        <li><strong>Pitch:</strong> After spectral subtraction against a Chamath-only noise reference (17–19s), pYIN recovered F0 traces in Friedberg's region that were absent from the raw audio. Gerstner's region showed none. Subtraction can create artifacts, so this is suggestive, not conclusive.</li>
        <li><strong>Cross-attention:</strong> Near zero in Friedberg's region. Low but present around Gerstner's, though that region borders active speech.</li>
        <li><strong>Speaker embeddings:</strong> Friedberg's ghost fell inside Chamath's local embedding space, suggesting the segment may have been absorbed into his turn rather than recognized as separate speech.</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="figure">
        <a href="https://pdf-sorcerer.github.io/alp/residual.png" target="_blank">
          <img
            src="https://pdf-sorcerer.github.io/alp/residual.png"
            alt="Libsyn spectral subtraction with pYIN F0"
          />
        </a>
        <p class="figure-caption">
          <em>pYIN F0 on raw vs. residual audio, t=20–30s; arrow marks F0 recovered after subtraction.</em>
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="figure">
        <a href="https://pdf-sorcerer.github.io/alp/attention_both_ghosts.png" target="_blank">
          <img
            src="https://pdf-sorcerer.github.io/alp/attention_both_ghosts.png"
            alt="Whisper cross-attention around both Ghost Audio regions"
          />
        </a>
        <p class="figure-caption">
          <em>Whisper cross-attention filtered to audio-aligned heads.</em>
        </p>
      </div>

      <p>
        <strong>Learning:</strong> Friedberg’s “Oh, my god.” omission could not be explained by an empty
        or missing audio region. After spectral subtraction, pYIN recovered pitch overlapping the timestamps
        where he spoke, while Whisper cross-attention showed only marginal, non-zero attention over the same
        region. The speech signal appeared to be present, but the model was barely attending to it.
      </p>

      <p>
        Gerstner’s line was never recovered by the acoustic analysis, so I drop that case from the
        investigation here and focus on Friedberg going forward.
      </p>

      <p>
        The question became: <strong>why wasn't it decoding the speech?</strong>
      </p>
    
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Decoder Fork</h2>

      <p>
        My production pipeline used CTranslate2, which didn't expose the decoder internals I needed
        to inspect. So I built a parallel PWW pipeline using Pyannote, reference Whisper, and Wav2vec
        to access cross-attention and token probabilities directly.
      </p>

      <p>
        At timestamp anchor <code>&lt;|18.98|&gt;</code>, hundreds of possible decoder
        paths opened up, but two mattered.
      </p>

      <div class="decoder-grid">
        <article>
          <p class="eyebrow">GHOST PATH · GROUND TRUTH</p>
          <h3>"Oh, my god."</h3>

          <table>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Oh</td><td>89.24%</td></tr>
              <tr><td>,</td><td>49.93%</td></tr>
              <tr><td>my</td><td>97.67%</td></tr>
              <tr><td>god</td><td>90.67%</td></tr>
              <tr><td>.</td><td>90.67%</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>

          <p><strong>Joint: 35.78%</strong></p>
        </article>

        <article>
          <p class="eyebrow">WINNER PATH · PRODUCTION OUTPUT</p>
          <h3>"He's not even here."</h3>

          <table>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>He</td><td>45.29%</td></tr>
              <tr><td>'s</td><td>99.94%</td></tr>
              <tr><td>not</td><td>99.96%</td></tr>
              <tr><td>even</td><td>99.98%</td></tr>
              <tr><td>here</td><td>99.78%</td></tr>
              <tr><td>.</td><td>92.01%</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>

          <p><strong>Joint: 41.53%</strong></p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <p>
        The decoder skipped Friedberg's earlier utterance and jumped ahead to his next spoken
        words. The ground-truth path lost by 5.75 percentage points.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="findings-section">
      <h2>A couple findings</h2>

      <ol class="finding-list">
        <li>
          <p>
            Token timestamps—not to be confused with word-level Wav2Vec timestamps—were sufficient
            to surface Friedberg's ghost in my production WhisperX pipeline. So, no PWW required,
            which was great because compute cost was about <strong>3 minutes vs. 9.5 minutes</strong>.
          </p>

          <details class="finding-accordion" open>
            <summary>
              <span>Production WHX beam=5 timestamp tokens</span>
            </summary>

            <div class="finding-body">
              <div class="timestamp-grid">
                <div class="timestamp-card">
                  <h3>Production WHX beam=5, Timestamp Tokens OFF</h3>

                  <div class="timestamp-content">
                    <p>
                      <strong>18.873–19.914</strong><br />
                      And the fattest, that's the dumbest.
                    </p>

                    <p class="timestamp-ghost">
                      <span class="ghost-icon timestamp-ghost-icon" aria-hidden="true">👻</span>
                      <span class="ghost-fade-text">
                        <strong>20.834–21.495</strong><br />
                        Oh, my god.
                      </span>
                    </p>

                    <p>
                      <strong>25.317–26.097</strong><br />
                      He's not even here.
                    </p>
                  </div>
                </div>

                <div class="timestamp-card">
                  <h3>Production WHX beam=5, Timestamp Tokens ON</h3>

                  <div class="timestamp-content">
                    <p>
                      <strong>18.873–19.914</strong><br />
                      And the fattest, that's the dumbest.
                    </p>

                    <p class="timestamp-recovered">
                      <strong>20.834–21.495</strong><br />
                      Oh, my god.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                      <strong>25.317–26.097</strong><br />
                      He's not even here.
                    </p>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </details>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            The custom pipeline was also tested at beam=1 and hallucinated text in the regions
            spanning Friedberg and Gerstner. The failures manifested as localized repetition and
            confabulation. With manual review, those failures can act as signals that a region
            deserves inspection—even when the missing speech itself is not recoverable, as with Gerstner.
          </p>

          <details class="finding-accordion" open>
            <summary>
              <span>PWW beam=1 Speech Hallucinations</span>
            </summary>

            <div class="finding-body">
              <p class="table-meta">
                Reference Whisper large-v3 · Pyannote decoder · Wav2Vec2 · beam=1 · 9.5 min GPU compute
              </p>

              <div class="finding-table-wrap">
                <table class="finding-table">
                  <thead>
                    <tr>
                      <th>Start</th>
                      <th>End</th>
                      <th>Text</th>
                      <th>Flag</th>
                    </tr>
                  </thead>

                  <tbody>
                    <tr>
                      <td>18.863</td>
                      <td>19.897</td>
                      <td>And the fattest, that's the dumbest.</td>
                      <td></td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                      <td>20.850</td>
                      <td>21.499</td>
                      <td>Oh, my god.</td>
                      <td></td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr class="row-repeat">
                      <td>22.046</td>
                      <td>23.100</td>
                      <td>Oh, my god.</td>
                      <td>⚠ localized repetition</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr class="row-halluc">
                      <td>23.444</td>
                      <td>25.020</td>
                      <td>I'm bullying when you're bullying.</td>
                      <td>⚠ localized hallucination</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                      <td>25.386</td>
                      <td>26.063</td>
                      <td>He's not even here.</td>
                      <td></td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                      <td>26.125</td>
                      <td>26.638</td>
                      <td>You can't do that.</td>
                      <td></td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                      <td>26.803</td>
                      <td>27.032</td>
                      <td>It's awful.</td>
                      <td></td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr class="row-halluc">
                      <td>27.364</td>
                      <td>28.902</td>
                      <td>I'm bullying when you're bullying.</td>
                      <td>⚠ localized hallucination</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                      <td>29.024</td>
                      <td>29.702</td>
                      <td>That's just bullying.</td>
                      <td></td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                      <td>29.723</td>
                      <td>30.077</td>
                      <td>It's awful.</td>
                      <td></td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr class="row-halluc">
                      <td>30.243</td>
                      <td>30.942</td>
                      <td>I'm bullying when you're bullying.</td>
                      <td>⚠ localized hallucination</td>
                    </tr>
                  </tbody>
                </table>
              </div>

              <p class="finding-note">
                The hallucinations above are a happy accident of the PWW pipeline's decoder.
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>BERD-1</h2>

      <p>
        BERD-1 operationalizes the first finding by comparing two production-equivalent
        WhisperX Beam=5 transcripts:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Timestamp Tokens OFF</strong> — <code>without_timestamps=True</code></li>
        <li><strong>Timestamp Tokens ON</strong> — <code>without_timestamps=False</code></li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        When the timestamp-enabled transcript contains speech absent from the
        timestamp-disabled transcript, BERD-1 generates a candidate repair for human review.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What I Found</h2>

      <ul>
        <li>Friedberg's ghost was successfully recovered</li>
        <li>BERD-1 surfaced roughly <strong>5–8 additional candidates</strong>, depending on settings</li>
        <li>most were short disfluencies such as <code>uh</code>, <code>ah</code>, and <code>right</code></li>
        <li>the custom Whisper pipeline produced localized hallucinations around otherwise difficult-to-transcribe speech</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        The corpus-wide impact remains unknown and is probably small.
      </p>

      <p class="closing">
        But Ghost Audio is no longer invisible. It can be surfaced, reviewed, and repaired.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>

